tree-sitter-ral
===============

A tree-sitter grammar for the ral shell language, used for editor
syntax highlighting via queries/highlights.scm.

The canonical source is grammar.js. Everything tree-sitter derives
from it is generated, not tracked: src/parser.c, src/grammar.json,
src/node-types.json, and the runtime headers under src/tree_sitter/
(parser.h, alloc.h, array.h). The compiled parser library (ral.dylib
and friends) and the node-gyp build/ tree are likewise build outputs.
All of these are listed in .gitignore.

Regeneration
------------

The package.json build script runs the generate step:

    npm run build        # tree-sitter generate && node-gyp build

or, to produce only the parser sources without compiling:

    tree-sitter generate

Run this after editing grammar.js to refresh src/parser.c and the
accompanying JSON and headers. Without node installed, evaluate
grammar.js with the CLI's own JS engine instead:

    tree-sitter generate --js-runtime native

Test the grammar against the behavioral corpus in test/corpus with
`tree-sitter test` (npm test). Check query compilation and captures with:

    tree-sitter query queries/highlights.scm path/to/sample.ral

Syntax contract and limits
--------------------------

The grammar follows docs/SPEC.md and core/src/syntax/{lexer,parser,numeral}.rs.
Bracket-context variants allow newlines without making them whitespace in
nested brace blocks. Immediate-start variants enforce ! and ... attachments;
postfix indices are adjacent, nonempty word keys. An indexed force operand
(!$x[k]) is distinct from indexing a forced result (!{f}[k]). Delimited names
($(name), !$(name)) take no postfix keys. The visible nodes retain ordinary
names through aliases; indexing uses indexed/deref/index nodes, with force
operands under the operand field.

This is an editor parser, not the runtime validator:

- Hash-bumped single strings support fence depths 1-3, not arbitrary depths.
  Use the regex bundles for highlighting arbitrary fences; runtime syntax
  itself has no fence-depth bound. Single-quoted strings have no escapes.
- Bare colon words use nonempty stems joined by colon runs (foo::bar).
  This is a deliberate approximation of the lexer's positional colon rule,
  not a second context-sensitive scanner.
- Numeral nodes recognize complete lexical shapes, including signs, partial
  decimal points and point-bearing exponents, not runtime numeric ranges.
- Brackets with spreads alone are lists. A keyed entry selects map_literal,
  which covers both records and maps. Classification-dependent record rules
  (one spread, first), duplicate keys/binders/arms, and types are not checked.
- Reserved control-operator heads other than let/return/if/case use application
  nodes. Highlight queries classify those names; this grammar does not check
  their form-specific option sets or arities.

() is unit; unit is text. Backslash is ordinary outside double quotes.
Pipelines and failure chains continue across blank/comment lines on either
side of | and ?, never across semicolons. Parentheses group only inside
$[...], apart from unit and delimited names.
